#
# The fifth trim narrowed L2/b3 to the banner and attached a condition to that narrowing: b3 is safe
# in the banner-only form ONLY while TRANSFORM is None and L1 is present and evaluated. Both hold at
# this cut (TRANSFORM is None below; L1 runs in check()), so the narrowed form is carried forward
# unchanged. A trim that ever needs a rebase must restore b3's records half in the same commit.
#
# ONE ASSERTION IS NEW, AND ONE INHERITED ARM IS STRENGTHENED (never relaxed).
#
#   L14 (LINEAGE FIGURES) — every ANCESTOR shard's span and size figure, in the four files L8
#      reaches, derived from each shard AT ITS OWN ADD-COMMIT and held against declared literals.
#
#      THE HOLE IS STRUCTURAL, NOT ACCIDENTAL, WHICH IS WHY IT SURVIVED SEVEN TRIMS. L12 asks
#      whether the sizes a trim states about ITS OWN cut are real; L13 asks the same of the spans.
#      Both take their measurements from `shard_recs` and `after_recs` — the two files in hand — so
#      both are scoped to the cut that ships them BY CONSTRUCTION. The moment the next trim lands,
#      the shard those assertions guarded becomes an ancestor, its figures fall out of every
#      assertion's reach, and they stay there forever. Seven trims' worth had accumulated: 44
#      figures in 31 literals, every one of them TRUE and every one of them read by nothing.
#      The seventh trim's own header measured this and bequeathed it; see the pointer block for why
#      its count of it (sixteen) was itself underived.
#
#      L14/set IS THE ARM THAT MATTERS MOST, and it is what stops the ninth trim reopening this.
#      The other arms check the literals a trim declares. L14/set checks which SHARDS those literals
#      are about, against the ancestor set ROUTING names. So when this cut's own shard becomes an
#      ancestor, a ninth trim that does not move its figures into L14 fails — it cannot inherit the
#      declaration unchanged and be green, which is exactly the state seven previous trims were in.
#
#      IT READS THE ADD-COMMIT BLOB, NEVER THE WORKING TREE. L9 proves disk == add-commit for every
#      ancestor, so reading disk would be *sufficient* today — and would make L14 silently depend on
#      L9 holding. `lineage` already carries the add-commit blob for each ancestor; L14 takes it.
#      An ancestor git knows no add-commit for is REPORTED, not skipped (M48 reaches that arm, and
#      trips L9 too — recorded below rather than contrived apart).
#
#      ITS LITERALS ARE DISJOINT FROM L8'S, L12'S AND L13'S. `BACKLOG.md`'s ordinal chain was
#      re-flowed so L8 keeps the ordinal words ("and the eighth,") and L14 takes the name-and-size
#      pairs; the plain-language index row was re-flowed the same way, so "785 for the eighth" is
#      L12's and "804, 933, 790, 976, 1,057 and 644 lines for the second through seventh" is L14's.
#      A string two assertions read gives the newer one no mutant of its own — the fourth trim's
#      finding, applied forward for the third trim running.
#
#   L13/census-unique — THE STRENGTHENING, and it is one arm, not two. The seventh trim gave L12 and
#      L13 a uniqueness arm over their PROSE literals after a review defeated a substring test with
#      a quotation three lines away. It did not give one to the CENSUS literals, which are membership
#      tests over the whole front matter — and this file makes that reachable: the seventh trim's own
#      pointer block, still in the front matter, contains "**eight declared sentences** stating which
#      sessions moved". This cut's census sentence is deliberately worded "**eight span sentences**"
#      so the two do not collide, and L13's census now asserts count == 1 rather than membership.
#      Wording around a collision is not a fix; the arm is.
#      L14/census-unique is the same arm, but it is NOT a strengthening — L14 did not exist before
#      this trim, so it ships with the arm rather than gaining one. The first draft of this header
#      said "TWO INHERITED ARMS ARE STRENGTHENED" and an adversarial review objected that only one
#      of the two is inherited. It was right, and the distinction is not pedantic: a reader counting
#      strengthenings to judge what changed would have counted this proof's own new code as a
#      hardening of the seventh's.
#
# AN ASSERTION WAS CONSIDERED AND REJECTED HERE, and as at the seventh trim the measurement is the
# reason. Every pointer block states a FILE CENSUS for its own sweep — "returns nineteen files",
# "returns nine files" — and nothing derives it. An L15 counting `git grep -l` hits was drafted.
# It was dropped because the count a block states is a measurement of the repository AT THAT
# TRIM'S PARENT, while the enumeration beside it describes the post-shard set: the seventh trim's
# "nineteen" is right at f26233a and its "seven shards with their seven proofs" is right at
# e7d5b03, and both are in one sentence. Mechanising either reading turns a defensible sentence
# red. What is needed first is a convention about WHICH commit a sweep count describes; that is a
# ruling, not an assertion, and inventing it inside a proof is how a proof teaches a session to
# type a false number. Recorded so the ninth trim rules on it rather than rediscovering it.
#
# A SECOND HOLE IS MEASURED HERE AND DELIBERATELY LEFT OPEN, named so it is inherited rather than
# rediscovered. `README.md`'s repo map carries one `.verify.sh` comment line per shard, describing
# which assertion that proof adds. EXACTLY ONE of the eight is read by anything: this trim's own,
# via L8/required. The other seven are guarded by nothing, and deleting one leaves all eight proofs
# green — L8/set cannot see it, because SHARD_NAME's negative lookahead deliberately makes a
# `.verify.sh` line not count toward the shard name set. That is L14's hole exactly, one field over.
# It is NOT closed here because it is a different KIND of field: those lines carry descriptive prose
# ("adds L10 (write-once for the ancestor PROOFS)"), not a figure anything can derive, so closing it
# means seven more hand-declared L8 strings rather than a derivation — more declaration surface to
# go stale, guarding a line whose referent L9 and L10 already hold byte-for-byte. Scope, not
# oversight: measured, priced, and declined in the same breath.
#
# MEASURED COVERAGE — every line below was produced by running the loops, not by predicting them.
# Neuter one assertion at a time and re-run --self-test; what survives is what it ALONE catches:
#
#     for A in L0 L1 L2 L3 L4 L5 L6 L7 L8 L9 L10 L11 L12 L13 L14; do
#       sed "s|^    fails += $A(.*|    pass|" <this> > /tmp/n.sh
#       echo "$A: $(bash /tmp/n.sh --self-test | grep SURVIVED)"; done
#
#     L0 (none)   L1 (none)*  L2 M12-M15,M40,M51   L3 (none)   L4 (none)
#     L5 M18-M26,M38,M39,M47,M52   L6 M27   L7 M29,M30   L8 M31-M34,M41,M43-M46,M50
#     L9 M35-M37   L10 M53-M55   L11 M56-M59,M84   L12 M60-M68,M82
#     L13 M69-M81,M83,M85   L14 M86-M95
#
#     * L1's blank entry is NOT a licence to remove it. L3 and L4 happen to catch every mutant L1
#       catches, so nothing depends on L1 ALONE — but L1 is the assertion the fifth trim's
#       narrowing of L2/b3 rests on, and b3's records half is dead only while L1 runs and
#       TRANSFORM is None. Read '(none)' here as 'overlapped', never as 'removable'.
#
#     THREE ENTRIES MOVED relative to the seventh trim, and each move is informative.
#     M12 is L2's ALONE here where it was shared there: that cut landed at exactly 1,050, so
#     restoring the longer stale paragraph pushed it over the declared target and L11 caught it
#     too; this cut lands at 1,014, so only L2 objects. M48 left L9's column: it now trips
#     L14/blob as well, because a shard with no add-commit cannot be measured. And L14 arrived
#     with ten mutants of its own, which is the plainest evidence available that it is not
#     decoration.
#
# THAT LOOP IS NOT ENOUGH, and this lineage has proved it at every trim that looked: the SECOND
# shipped L2/b0 reachable by no mutant, the THIRD shipped L5/2's partition arm, an adversarial sweep
# of the FOURTH found four more, the SIXTH found two of its own, the SEVENTH found one by sweep and
# three more by adversarial review, and the EIGHTH — this one — shipped L14 declaring 44 ancestor
# figures when 45 were present, which no mutant and no arm sweep could see because every arm it had
